    "['mpls te bandwidth max-reservable-bandwidth [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls traffic-engineering bandwidth-attributes maximum-reservable-bw 5000', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Juniper equivalent command for the given HUAWEI configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls traffic-engineering bandwidth-attributes maximum-reservable-bw 5000\\n##\\n\\nThis Juniper command sets the maximum reservable bandwidth for MPLS TE to 5000 kbps, which corresponds to the HUAWEI \"mpls te bandwidth max-reservable-bandwidth 5000\" command.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls traffic-engineering bandwidth-attributes maximum-reservable-bw 5000'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls te bandwidth bc0 [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls interface ge-2/0/0 bandwidth 4000', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper equivalent command for `mpls te bandwidth bc0 4000`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls interface ge-2/0/0 bandwidth 4000\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, MPLS TE bandwidth is configured under `protocols mpls` for the specific interface\\n- `bc0` refers to the default bandwidth constraint (BC0) in Huawei, which maps directly to the basic bandwidth reservation in Juniper\\n- The value `4000` (kbps) remains the same, just configured in Juniper's syntax\\n- The interface name is converted to Juniper's naming convention (ge-2/0/0 instead of GigabitEthernet2/0/0)\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls interface ge-2/0/0 bandwidth 4000'}]]": 1,
    "['ip address unnumbered interface [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces Tunnel10 unit 0 family inet unnumbered-address lo0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the HUAWEI command `ip address unnumbered interface LoopBack1`:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces Tunnel10 unit 0 family inet unnumbered-address lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, the `unnumbered-address` parameter is used instead of \"unnumbered interface\"\\n- `lo0.0` refers to the loopback interface (equivalent to LoopBack1 in HUAWEI)\\n- The command is applied under the tunnel interface\\'s inet family configuration\\n- The unit number (0) is explicitly specified as Juniper requires interface units to be defined', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces Tunnel10 unit 0 family inet unnumbered-address lo0.0'}]]": 1,
    "['tunnel-protocol mpls te', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls interface all', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols mpls interface all\\n##\\n\\nThe `tunnel-protocol mpls te` command in Huawei configures an interface to use MPLS Traffic Engineering (TE) tunneling protocol. In Juniper, the equivalent is to enable MPLS on the interface (which includes TE capabilities) using the `set protocols mpls interface` command. \\n\\nNote: Juniper handles MPLS TE differently - you would typically configure the LSP separately under `protocols mpls` hierarchy rather than at the interface level. The exact TE parameters would be configured in Juniper's MPLS LSP configuration section.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls interface all'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls te signal-protocol rsvp-te', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls te-tunnels signaling rsvp', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols mpls te-tunnels signaling rsvp\\n##', 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls te-tunnels signaling rsvp'}]]": 1,
    "['destination [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel10 to 3.3.3.9', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel10 to 3.3.3.9\\n##\\n\\nThe Juniper equivalent of the HUAWEI `destination 3.3.3.9` command in the context of an MPLS TE tunnel configuration is the `to` statement under the label-switched-path configuration, specifying the destination LSR ID (3.3.3.9 in this case).', 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls label-switched-path Tunnel10 to 3.3.3.9'}]]": 1,
